== Children Honoring Father and Mother in the Lord

=== §. 52. #emph[Of the manner of performing childrens duties.]

That clause which #footnote[§ 37.] noted out the #emph[limitation] of childrens duties, affordeth also an excellent #emph[direction] for the #emph[manner] of performing them. It is this, #emph[in the Lord:] that is, #emph[children must so perform their duty to their parents as they would or should perform it to the Lord.]

More particularly it implieth these six points: #footnote[How children obey their parents #emph[in the Lord.]]

1. That their duties be performed #emph[in conscience,] or #emph[for conscience sake,] which is all one as #emph[for the Lords sake:] for the Lord only is Judge of the conscience and hath power over it. Thus #footnote[1\. By obeying #emph[for the Lords sake. Rom.] 13. 5. 1 #emph[Pet.] 2. 13. #emph[Col.] 3. 20.] must subjects perform duty to magistrates: much more children to parents. The reason which the Apostle rendereth in these words, #emph[This is well-pleasing to the Lord,] showeth that children in obeying their parents must labour to approve themselves to God.

2. Their duties must be in sincerity, which is when children #footnote[2 In sincerity.] pretend in show to do no more, then in truth and heart they mean: #emph[Whatsoever ye do, do it heartily as to the Lord,] saith #footnote[#emph[Col.] 3. 23.] the Apostle. Parents use to deal with none more heartily then with their children: accordingly must children deal with parents.

3. They must be performed cheerfully, with a willing and #footnote[3\. With cheerfulness.] ready mind: for the Lord loveth cheerfulness. Herein lyeth a main difference betwixt a filial and servile, a child #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 9. 7.] like and slavelike obedience. #footnote[4 With revetence.]

4. They must be performed reverendly as to them which bear the Image of God. Hereof we spake #footnote[§. 3.] before.

5. They must so be performed, as in performing them no #footnote[5\. In the fear of God.] sin be committed against God. Hereof also we spake #footnote[§. 37. & 38.] before.

6. Constancy must be added to all other virtues. For as the #footnote[6\. With perseverance.] Lord himself is constant in all his ways and works, so he expecteth that children should be in the duties which he requireth at their hands. He that begineth well, and holdeth not on, loseth all the glory of his good beginning.

If the examples of all good children commended in Scripture be well weighed, we shall find their duties, so far forth as they were acceptable to God, performed after the foresaid #emph[manner,] in all the branches thereof.

=== §. 53. #emph[Of the aberrations of children in the manner of their obedience.]

Contrary are these aberrations.

1. When children perform their duties on by-respects: #footnote[Faults in the manner of doing duty. 1. By-respects.] for fear of parents wrath, and the punishment following thereon: for hope and expectation of greater portion and allowance: upon instant persuasion of friends, with the like: these respects simply in themselves are not #emph[for the Lord.]

2. When they perform them only outwardly in show, #footnote[2\. Complemental.] complementally while parents are in presence, or may know thereof. This is not with respect to God who seareheth the heart.

3. When they perform them grudgingly, mutteringly, #footnote[3\. Rudely.] disdainfully: as if their parents authority were an usurped power and not given them of God. Is this #emph[in the Lord?]

4. When they perform them rudely and unmannerly: #footnote[4\. Grudgingly.] his showeth they consider not the glory of Gods Image shining in their parents.

5. #footnote[§. 38.] When they care not how they sin against God, so #footnote[5\. Only to please man.] they may please their parents.

6. When, as if they repented of what they have well done, #footnote[6\. Unsetledly.] they refuse to do any more duty to their parents. They wax weary, thinking that God hath laid too heavy a burden upon them. Many show themselves more dutiful in their younger then in their riper years. That which maketh children weary in doing duty, is commonly the great and long need of their parents, as long sickness, long impotency, long poverty, with the like. It appears that such children look only on their parents as men, which, as they imagine, can never recompense their pains and cost: they look not to God who is able abundantly to recompense all. These therefore perform not their duty #emph[in the Lord.]

=== §. 54. #emph[Of the equal respect that children are to bear to both parents.]

As the distinct duties of children have been set forth, so I think it requisite to declare distinctly who the parties be to whom those duties are to be performed. These principally are the natural parents, both #emph[father] and #emph[mother.] Secondarily such as are in the place of parents.

The first point then to be noted is, that children bear an equal respect to both their natural parents, and perform duty to both alike. The law expressly mentioneth both, #emph[honour thy father and thy mother.] Well may we think that there was some just and urgent cause, that the law which so briefly under as few words as well could be, compriseth exceeding much matter, should expressly mention #emph[father] and #emph[mother,] when as there is one word, #emph[parent,] which includeth both. Now what other reason can be rendered then the point in hand?

It is worthy to be noted, how the Apostle contenteth not himself to have named #emph[parents,] which implieth both, but also annexeth the express words of the law, which in particular setteth down, #emph[father and mother.] It is expressly set down of #emph[Jacob] that he obeyed #emph[his father and his mother.] Among other #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 28. 7.] pen-men of Scripture #emph[Solomon] expressly mentioneth #emph[both father and mother,] even almost twenty several times in Prov.

Many reasons there be to enforce this point.

1. Both parents are under God a like means of their childrens #footnote[Reasons.] being. Children come out of the substance of both alike.

2. The care and pains of both for the good of the children is very great: I know not of whether the greater. The mothers pains and care in bringing forth the child is indeed the greater, and it may be also the greater in bringing up the child, especially while it is young, at least if she give it suck her self: yet afterwards the fathers exceedeth in providing fit calling, sufficient means of maintenance, yea and portion or inheritance for it, and that after he himself is dead. Thus one way or other the child is equally bound to both: and accordingly Gods law maketh no difference betwixt them.

#strong[#emph[Object.] The wife is subject to her husband: therefore a child ought to prefer his father before his mother.]

#emph[Answ.] Though there be a difference betwixt father and #footnote[Both parents to be of like account with children.] mother in relation of one to another, yet in relation to their children they are both as one, and have a like authority over them. Now children are not to look to that difference that is betwixt their parents in that mutual relation that is betwixt husband and wife, but to that authority which both parents have over their children: and so to carry an equal respect to both.

#strong[2. #emph[Object.] What if the fathers and mothers disposition #footnote[Direction for a child when one parent commandeth one thing, the other another.] be contrary: and the one command what the other forbids.]

#emph[Answ.] The thing commanded or forbidden must be observed: if it be about a thing simply good or evil, lawful, or unlawful, then the parent which would have the thing lawful to be done, or unlawful to be for borne (though it be the mother) must be obeyed: for in this case she is backt with Gods authority. But if the matter be merely indifferent, then I doubt not but the father must be obeyed: yet so as the child no way show any contempt to his mother, but with all reverence and humility make it known to her that it is best both for her-self and himself, that his father be obeyed. But if the fathers contrary authority be not interposed, or if the father be dead, then is a mother as simply and absolutely to be obeyed in all things, as a father.

=== §. 55. #emph[Of pretences alleged to obey father rather then mother.]

Contrary on the one side is their childish fondness who so wholly respect their mother, and seek to please her, as they little regard their father, unless through fear they be forced thereto: and on the other side, their scornful spirit who only bear respect to their father, and altogether neglect their mother, if not despise her. For the most part, of the two, the mother is less regarded. The reasons whereof I take to be these following, whereunto I will annex particular means to remove the seeming force of those reasons, as antidotes or remedies unto them.

1. The mother by reason of her sex is commonly the weaker, and subject to more infirmities.

#emph[Answ.] children ought rather to look upon their mothers place and authority, then their person and infirmity: so #emph[these] would no whit impair their respect of the #emph[other.]

2. The mother is more indulgent and tender to her children: and useth more familiarity towards them: now #emph[familiarity] #footnote[Familiaritas parit contemptum. Teren. in Andr.] #emph[breedeth contempt.]

#emph[Answ.] This is the abuse of familiarity: love should breed love: and love must be ordered according to the condition of the parties loving, and loved. As children with one eye behold the affection of the mother, so with another they should behold Gods image in the mother; and then (that which is #footnote[§. 2, 3.] noted as the ground of all childrens duty) a #emph[loving-fear] would be wrought in their heart, which would cast out all contempt.

3. The mother hath not that power to reward or revenge that a father hath.

#emph[Answ.] 1. This is not to obey #emph[in the Lord.] No outward respect should move the child to obey his parents so much as conscience to God-wards. If children duly considered God, how he hath made no difference, but commanded them to obey both alike, and how he is able abundantly to reward, and severely to revenge, that reason would be no reason.

4. The mother is subject to the father.

#emph[Answ.] This was removed #footnote[§. 54.] before.

As a general answer to these and all other such pretences as can be alleged, let it be noted that the Lord doth not only in express terms charge children to #emph[fear their mother,] but also (the more to press this point) sometime setteth the mother in the first place, thus; #emph[Ye shall fear every man his mother,] #footnote[#emph[Leu.] 19. 3.] #emph[and his father:] which is not so to be taken as if the mother of the two were the more excellent (in this respect the father for the most part is first named) but because it is the truest trial of a childes subjection to be subject to his mother. He that willingly and conscionably subjecteth himself to her, who is in relation to her husband the inferior, in sex the weaker, in condition subject to more infirmities, in her affections less moderate, in power less able to reward, or to punish, will much rather (no doubt) subject himself to his father.

=== §. 56. #emph[Of the difference of childrens subjection to natural parents, and to such as only are in the place of parents.]

Beside #emph[natural parents,] there are others by God so set over children, as they owe in conscience child-like duty unto them. These in general are such as are in the place of parents, and so perform, or at least ought to perform, the duty of parents to children.

They are of two sorts.

1. Such as are joined to a natural parent in marriage, commonly called #emph[Step-fathers, and step-mothers,] or #emph[fathers in law, and mothers in law.]

2. Such as have the government and tuition of children committed to them, commonly called #emph[Guardians, Tutors, Governors:] yea also #emph[foster-fathers, and foster-mothers.]

#strong[#emph[Quest.] Is the same duty in every respect due to those who are in place of parents, as to natural parents themselves?]

#emph[Answ.] Not so. There is such a prerogative appertaining to natural parents from whom children have received their being, that many things wherein children by an absolute necessity #footnote[De necessitate.] are bound to them, are bound unto the other who are but in place of parents, only by the law of honesty, of meetness & #footnote[De honestate.] conueniency. Whence it cometh to pass that such things as being done by children rebelliously without, or against the consent of natural parents prove mere nullities, will stand in force though they be done without or against the consent of such as are only in the place of parents: instance contracts made about goods, lands and such other things as parents retain a right in: whereunto many good Divines add #footnote[See §. 16.] contracts of marriage, of calling and the like.

Yet notwithstanding seeing in conscience we are bound to those things which the law of honesty, and conueniency requirety (for #emph[whatsoever things are honest, just, and of good report,] #footnote[#emph[Phil.] 4. 8.] are to be done) great is that duty which children owe to those who are in place of parents.

=== §. 57. #emph[Of childrens subjection to fathers and mothers in law.]

Fathers and mothers in law are to be ranked in the first degree of those who are in the place of natural parents. Very good proof there is in Scripture for childrens subjection to them. The respect which #emph[Moses] bear to his father in law, #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 18. 7. #emph[Ruth.] 1. & 2. #emph[Luk.] 2. 51.] and #emph[Ruth] to her mother in law, and #emph[Christ] himself to his supposed father, are commended for this very purpose. #emph[subjection] is noted in Christs example: #emph[reverence] in #emph[Moses: Recompense] in #emph[Ruths.]

The marriage bond maketh man and wife #emph[one flesh:] whereupon #footnote[1\. #emph[Reason.]] the natural parent that marieth another, maketh that other #emph[one with him,] and in that respect is as a parent to the children, and by them ought to be accounted so.

The law of God maketh it plain #emph[incest] for a son to #emph[uncover] #footnote[#emph[Leu.] 18. 8, 17.] #emph[the shame of his mother in law,] or for a father in law to #emph[uncover the shame of his daughter in law.] Whereby it appeareth that fathers and mothers in law, are by Gods law in the very place and steed of natural parents to their children, and accordingly as natural parents are to be honoured.

Besides it is a great honour which a child doth to his natural #footnote[2\. #emph[Reason.]] father or mother to respect such as they have made one flesh with themselves, as they do their own parent. So as to honour a father, or mother in law, is to honour a natural parent.

Contrary is the mind and carriage of most children. Very few bear a reverend, dutiful, and child-like respect to stepfathers, and step-mothers: but for the most part despise them in heart, grumble at them in speech, and are very undutiful in their behaviour: whence it cometh to pass that they bring much grief to their natural parent, and oft cause much discord and dissention betwixt their natural, and step-parents being herein set on work by Satan, who laboureth what he can to disunite those whom God hath nearly united together and made one flesh. Lamentable experience showeth that the #emph[second, third,] or any #emph[after-marriages] are seldom so comfortable and peaceable as the #emph[first:] especially if either the #emph[one,] or #emph[other,] or #emph[both] have children. The cause thereof, for the most part, is in children, who brooke not fathers or mothers in law.

#strong[#emph[Object.] Nature cannot so well brooke a step-parent, as a natural parent.]

#emph[Answ.] 1. If not so well, yet despise them not: there is a difference betwixt extremes.

2. Conscience and religion ought to alter corrupt nature. Look to Gods ordinance: and let Gods fear possess thine heart, that that may bring thee to do what nature cannot.

#strong[2. #emph[Object.] Fathers and Mothers in law seldom respect their husbands or wives former children.]

#emph[Answ.] This objection more beseemeth the mouth of a barbarian then a Christian. The Gospel teacheth to #emph[be subject not] #footnote[1 #emph[Pet.] 2. 18. #emph[Rom.] 12. 21.] #emph[only to the good and gentle, but also to the froward:] and #emph[not to be overcome of evil, but to overcome evil with goodness.]

The Scripture reckoneth such as rise up against their parents #footnote[#emph[Mic.] 7. 6.] in law, among such as rise up against their natural parents: showing thereby that the impiety of #emph[those,] is as blame-worthy, as the impiety of #emph[these.]

=== §. 58. #emph[Of childrens subjection to Guardians, Tutors, etc.]

For childrens subjection to Governors, Guardians, and Tutors, the Apostle saith that a child is #emph[under] them. If by the law #footnote[#emph[Galat.] 4. 2.] children are #emph[under] them, they ought in equity to be subject to them. It is expressly noted of #emph[Esther,] even after the King had chosen her for his wife, that she #emph[did the commandment of Mordecai] #footnote[#emph[Esth.] 2. 20.] (who was as a Guardian to her) #emph[like as when she was brought up with him.] A memorable pattern for this purpose. What pretences might she have made to have cast off all manner of subjection? #emph[Mordecai] was but her cosen: she was now advanced above him: a wife she was, and so subject to her husband: yea a Kings wife, and so her self a Queen: yet she was subject, and her subjection commended. We read that the children of the Prophets much #footnote[2 #emph[Kin.] 2. 15, #emph[etc. &] 6. 1.] respected #emph[Elisha,] did reverence to him, and were obedient to him, because he was as a father and tutor unto them: in which respect also #emph[Elisha] was as a child subject to #emph[Eliah,] whom he called #footnote[2 #emph[King.] 2. 12.] #emph[father.]

Guardians, Tutors, and such like over-seers of children, have a parents charge laid upon them, as we shall #footnote[Treat 6. §. 74, etc.] after show: great reason therefore that child-like duty should be yielded to them. Their honour must be answerable to their charge and care.

Contrary is the conceit of many lawless children, whose parents being dead, or they being sent and placed abroad out of the government of their parents, think they are clean free from all government, and may live as they list. Their practise being correspondent to this opinion, they run into all riot, and in time bring themselves to utter ruin. It is the overthrow of many children, that they regard not their Guardians, Tutors, and Governors: and God in judgment bringeth many such refractary children to some fearful & shameful end or other. Let children therefore be wise, and not take too much liberty to themselves.

Thus far have been handled the duties of children in their several #emph[kinds,] the #emph[manner] of performing them, and the #emph[parties] to whom they are to be performed. It remaineth to speak of the reasons which the Apostle allegeth to enforce those duties.

=== §. 59. #emph[Of the] place #emph[of parents, whereby children ought to be moved to obey them.]

The reasons which the Apostle useth to move children to perform their duty are four:

- The #emph[first] is taken from the #emph[place] of the parent.
- The #emph[second] from the #emph[equity] of the thing.
- The #emph[third] from #emph[Gods] express #emph[charge.]
- The #emph[fourth] from #emph[Gods promise.]

I. The #emph[place] of a parent is noted forth by this phrase, IN THE LORD: which as it affordeth a #footnote[. 37.] #emph[limitation,] and giveth a #footnote[§. 52.] #emph[direction] to children, so it addeth a spur unto them, to stir them up to perform their duty: for it showeth that parents are to then children #emph[in the Lords steed:] as an evidence thereof hath God #footnote[Liberis suis pater Dei vice est. Greg. Naz. ad Vitit.] communicated to them this glorious and honourable title (father) which is #footnote[See §. 5.] proper and peculiar to himself. Now then parents being to their children in #emph[Gods steed,] and by virtue of their place bearing #emph[Gods image,] children in obeying their parents, obey God; in refusing to obey them, refuse to obey God. A strong motive is this first motive. For who is so void of religion, but will think it most meet, that God should be honoured? Or who so impious, as to refuse to yield duty to God? Here then children may learn in one main point how to honour the Lord, & show themselves children of God. And let them here by know, that disobedient and undutiful children, that resist the authority of parents, #emph[resist the ordinance of God, and receive to themselves] #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 13. 2.] #emph[damnation.]

=== §. 60. #emph[Of the equity whereby children should be moved to obey their parents.]

II. The second reason in these words, #emph[for this is right,] though it be not of greater force, yet is it more plainly and expressly noted to be a reason, as the causal particle #emph[(for)] prefixed before it, showeth. It is a general reason drawn from common equity; a reason which may move all sorts, even Infidels and Pagans: therefore much more Christians.

In saying, #emph[It is right,] he implieth three things.

1. That it is agreeable to all law. To the law of #emph[God,] which #footnote[#emph[Right] implieth three things. 1. It is agreeable to all law.] is the fountain of equity. To the law of #emph[nature,] which proceeds from thence. And to the law of #emph[Nations,] which is answerable in many respects. The forenamed proofs out of Gods word, show it to be agreeable to #emph[Gods law.] The precepts which heathen men have given of this point, show it to be agreeable to the #emph[law of nature.] And the many Constitutions of Law-makers, in all ages, show it to be agreeable to the #emph[law of nations.]

2. That the place of parents requireth as much. For RIGHT #footnote[2\. It is due by virtue of parents place.] requireth that every one have his own, that which is his due. It being therefore #emph[right,] that children obey their parents, that duty is due to them.

3. That parents deserve as much: for #emph[right] presupposeth #emph[desert.] #footnote[3\. It is a debt.] That which is done #emph[of right,] is done by way of #emph[recompense.] The cause of this desert, is the love which parents bear to children, the paine, pains, care, and cost they have been at, in bringing them forth, and bringing them up.

These things being implied under #emph[right,] note what followeth from the force of this reason,

1. Disobedient and undutiful children, transgress against #emph[Gods law,] against #emph[common equity] of all nations, and against #emph[light of nature:] so as they are unworthy of the name of Christians, they are worse then Infidels, yea worse then the brute beasts.

2. They have no respect to the place of their parents, they disgrace the image of God, which their parents by virtue of their place carry, and they disturb that comely order and degree, which God hath set among men.

3. They ill repay their parents care and pains for their good, and show themselves both #emph[ungrateful] and #emph[unnatural:] which are two most monstrous and odious sins.

=== §. 61. #emph[Of Gods accepting childrens obedience.]

The forenamed reason is by this same Apostle in another place thus set down, #emph[This is well-pleasing to the Lord.] By comparing #footnote[#emph[Coloss.] 3. 20.] that place with my text, I observe, that

#emph[What is right is well-pleasing to the Lord.] And again, #emph[What is well-pleasing to the Lord is right.]

These two propositions, are (as we speak in Schools) #emph[convertible] and #emph[reciprocal;] either of them true of the other every way. Wherefore the Apostle, in another place, joyneth them both together. For having exhorted children to recompense their parents, he saith, #emph[That is good and acceptable before God.] #emph[Good] or #footnote[1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 4.] #emph[honest] is in effect the same that #emph[right.]

This showeth, that dutiful children, as they do that which is good, or honest, and right unto their parents; so also therein they highly please God: so as they may be sure not to lose thereby. At Gods hand they shall receive a reward, though their parents should little regard their dutifulness. Thus God dealt with #emph[Jacob.] His father in law ill repaid his faithfulness: but God looked #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 7, #emph[etc. vers] 42.] upon him, and abundantly recompensed all his pains.

This showeth again, that undutiful children, as they do not that which is right, but rather wrong unto their parents; so they highly displease God: they may be sure therefore not to escape unpunished, though their parents, through over-much indulgency, should let them alone: instance the two impious children of #emph[Ely, Hophni] and #emph[Phinehas:] and the two rebellious children of #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 2. 34. 2 #emph[Sam.] 18. 9. 1 #emph[King.] 2. 25.] #emph[David, Absalom,] and #emph[Adoniah.]

This therefore addeth force to the second reason, and moveth children to do #emph[right] to their parents, by obeying them, as they respect God, his #emph[good pleasure,] or #emph[displeasure:] as they look for #emph[recompense,] or fear #emph[vengeance] from him.

=== §. 62. #emph[Of Gods charge, how inviolable a bond it is to tie children to obey their parents.]

III. The #emph[third] drawn from #emph[Gods charge] is not only in express terms noted, but for more weight the very words of the moral law are quoted #emph[(honour thy father and thy mother:)] and it is added, that this is a #emph[commandment,] yea the #emph[first] commandment, and that #emph[with promise,] all which is for amplification of the point.

Here then consider we

+ The reason it self.
+ The amplification thereof.

From the reason that #emph[it is Gods charge,] I gather these three points.

1. That it is no arbitrary matter left to childrens discretion #footnote[Childrens obedience to parents is, 1. No arbitrary thing.] whether they will obey their parents or no: but a matter whereunto in conscience they are bound, even as they owe alleageance to God himself the great law-giver: so as in neglecting duty to parents, children sin even against God himself: for the same God who hath commanded us to honour himself, hath commanded us to #emph[honour father and mother.]

2. That this is a #emph[perpetual] and a #emph[general] law: a law whereunto #footnote[2\. An eternal law, and general to all children.] all children of all times, places, sexes, estates and degrees always have been, still are, and ever shall be bound. For the moral law is of that extent: it is #emph[general] for all persons: #emph[perpetual] for all times. Therefore no children may think to be exempted from it.

#strong[Quest. What if children be grown to years, and not under their parents government?]

Answ. There may be some difference both in some #emph[parts] of subjection, and also in the #emph[manner] of subjection betwixt such children as remain under their parents government, and such as are freed from it: but yet so long as a child hath a parent, of what age or estate so ever he be, he oweth an #emph[honour] unto his parent. #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 47. 12. #emph[&] 48. 12. #emph[&] 50. 5.] #emph[Joseph] when he was a great Governor of Egypt, a man grown, having wife and children, yet performed the honour of #emph[reverence, obedience,] and #emph[recompense] unto his father. #footnote[1 #emph[King.] 2. 19. 3. Indispensable.] #emph[Solomon] when he was in an higher place then #emph[Joseph,] even an absolute King, did the honour of #emph[reverence] to his mother.

3. That no man can give children any dispensation, or ex emption from their duty to parents. If any may, he must be either the law-maker himself, or one greater then he, one that hath power over him. Now God is the author and giver of that law, #emph[God spake all these words,] etc. But is any greater then #footnote[#emph[Exo.] 20. 1.] God? Hath any power over him? Who then can dispense with children in this kind?

Hence then I infer these two conclusions.

1. That the Popes dispensation is nothing worth: they who make it to be of force, must make him greater then God, which is a horrible blasphemy.

2. Though parents themselves should not exact duty of their children, yet children were bound to perform duty to them, because of Gods charge. To this therefore are children to look. This showeth that there is no less paine hanging over the heads of the transgressors of this charge, then the curse of the law, eternal condemnation.

=== §. 63. #emph[Of the title] FIRST #emph[given to the fifth commandment.]

In the amplification of the forenamed reason, the Apostle styleth the commandment of honouring parents the #emph[first with promise,] as to show that it is the first which hath a particular promise annexed to it; so it is the first that God gave of any duty to be performed unto man. The very order of the decalogue manifesteth the truth hereof. The reason is clear: honour due to parents is the ground of all the duties required in the second table: for if duty be not performed to such as we are bound unto by some peculiar bond, may we think that it will be performed to such as we are bound unto at large? Now of all to whom we are first and most bound, and to whom we owe our first duty, our parents are the persons. They therefore who are rebellious against their parents, and refuse to do their duty to them, will hardly perform duty to any other. Little hope that a disobedient child will prove a profitable member in Church or common-wealth. #emph[Absalom,] who was a rebellious child, proved but a traitorous subject: and #emph[Hophni] and #emph[Phineas] that refused to hearken to the voice of their father, proved but sacrilegious Priests. Wherefore if any precept of the second table be conscionably to be observed (as all are, for the same law-maker gave all, and Christ hath said that #emph[the second table is like the first)] then is this of #emph[honouring father] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 22. 39.] #emph[and mother,] among the rest, and above the rest to be observed, at least if difference of observing any may be made,

But this particle #emph[(first)] being set down, not simply but with a connection of promise with it #emph[(The first with promise)] we are duly to consider the promise thereof: whence a fourth reason ariseth.

=== §. 64. #emph[Of Gods promise moving children to obey their parents.]

The #emph[fourth] reason taken from #emph[Gods promise,] is both generally #emph[propounded] and particularly #emph[exemplified.] Propounded in this clause #emph[(first with promise.)] Exemplified in the third verse.

For the #emph[general.] Gods promise made to the performance of any duty, cannot but be a strong motive to stir us up to perform it. Men hereby do stir up, and provoke one another #footnote[Promise of reward a great motive.] to perform any thing. Thus Kings when they would fain have their subjects do this or that, promise such and such rewards unto them: Thus masters incite their servants, parents their children, and one man another. If the promises of men encourage us to perform the things which they give us in charge, how much more ought the promise of God? Men #footnote[How Gods promise is a stronger motive then any mans.] are deceitful, and may deal doubly, pretending one thing with their mouths, and intending another with their heart, and never mean to perform what they promise: But God is faithful and true: his words are as deeds: his promises as performances; so as he never maketh show of more then he means to perform. Again, mans power is limited; though he truly intend what he promiseth, yet in the performance he may fail either in that he knew not his own power, but thought when he made the promise he could have done more then in the event he findeth he can do; or in that he is after wards by some occasion hindered, or disabled. But Gods power cannot be so straitned, or hindered. Besides, men may be taken away before the time of performing their promise is come: but God ever lieth, and changeth not. If then mans promises be any moves to any thing, much more Gods who ever remaineth the same. Betwixt God and man there is no proportion, no comparison. #footnote[How Gods promise commendeth Gods love.]

This motive doth exceedingly commend Gods fatherly indulgency towards us, and the earnest desire he hath of our good. For he hath such power and authority over all his creatures, that the very knowledge of his will ought to provoke them to perform any duty which he shall command: and if they obey not, he might presently execute vengeance upon them. But considering that we are his children, and need many allurements to draw us on by little and little, he accordingly dealeth with us. He standeth not wholly and only upon his authority, but addeth promises thereto: (for #emph[this is a commandment with promise.)] If notwithstanding all this, children refuse to obey their parents, may not the Lord justly expostulate the matter with them, as sometimes in another case he did with the Israelites, and say, #emph[Judge between me and these children:] #footnote[#emph[Isa.] 5. 3, 4.] #emph[what could I have done more that I have not done? I gave them an express charge to honour their parents: I laid it down in the first place as a main and principal charge: to encourage them to keep it, I added a promise of good to redound to themselves: what could I do more? Do not they justly deserve vengeance that regard none of these?] Thus, in that #emph[this is a commandment with promise,] we see how children disobedient to their parents are both #emph[rebellious against God] in regard of the #emph[commandment] which they transgress, and #emph[injurious to themselves] in regard of the #emph[promise] which they make to be void and of no effect. Of this particular promise see more in the first treatise, §. 97, 98, etc.
